ground|surface-of-earth|the solid surface of the earth where people may stand|sit on the ground; wet ground|A|Oxford examples describe people sitting on earth and rain softening it; keep this common physical noun distinct from grounds meaning a property.
ground|institutional-grounds|the land or outdoor area belonging to a school or other institution|school grounds; grounds are open|B|Oxford marks the plural form grounds for an institution’s land; its use differs from singular ground as the earth surface.
identify|determine-identity|find out or recognize who or what a person or thing is|identify a person in a photo; identify a bird|A|Both sources describe finding out what something is; Oxford’s person and bird examples support one transitive verb meaning.
constantly|repeatedly-or-continuously|at frequent intervals or without stopping over a period|constantly updated; ring constantly|A|Oxford supports frequent ringing and ongoing updates, while Vocab4600 says continuous basis; combine these closely related adverbial uses without claiming a separate meaning.
crust|bread-or-pizza-outer-layer|the firm outer part of bread or a baked pizza|bread crust; pizza crust|A|Vocab4600 directly describes a loaf’s tough outside and its example mentions pizza; one food-related noun sense covers both.
personality|character-and-behavior|the qualities and usual behavior that make a person distinctive|friendly personality; calm personality|A|Oxford examples show friendly and calm traits; Vocab4600 describes what a person is like and how they behave.
conclude|infer-from-evidence|reach a judgment after considering facts or information|conclude that something is true; conclude from evidence|A|Both sources directly support reasoning from evidence; Oxford’s researchers and report examples establish this clause-taking verb use.
conclude|bring-to-an-end|finish an event or activity|conclude a meeting; conclude with a discussion|B|Oxford separately shows concluding a meeting with discussion; this transitive verb meaning is an ending action rather than an inference.
incidence|rate-of-occurrence|the rate at which an event, condition, or problem happens|incidence of injuries; incidence of errors|A|Both sources define a rate and Oxford applies it to injuries and errors; avoid treating incidence as the count of individual cases.
tolerant|accepting-differences|willing to respect or accept other people’s beliefs or behavior|tolerant of beliefs; tolerant of habits|A|Vocab4600 explicitly supports respect for rights and beliefs and its example applies tolerant to habits; one adjective meaning covers both attitudes.
boss|person-in-charge-at-work|a person who directs or supervises other people at work|my boss; a boss thanks a team|A|Both sources describe a person in charge at work; Oxford examples show workplace permissions and thanks, supporting one countable noun sense.
secret|adjective-kept-private|kept hidden from other people or not publicly known|secret code; secret meeting|A|Oxford’s secret code illustrates the adjective; it describes information or an activity concealed from others.
secret|noun-private-information|information known to a limited group and not shared openly|keep a secret; share a secret|B|Oxford and Vocab4600 support a piece of information not told to others; retain the noun separately from the adjective.
authority|power-or-right|the official power or right to make decisions or enforce rules|authority to approve; authority to regulate|A|Both sources describe decision-making power and Oxford illustrates approval; this abstract noun is distinct from an organization.
authority|official-body|a government or public organization responsible for a subject or area|local authority; health authority|B|Oxford directly supports local and health authorities as bodies; the institutional noun use is different from having authority or power.
stability|state-of-being-steady|a condition of remaining steady or not changing unexpectedly|political stability; stability of a lamp|A|Both sources describe steadiness and lack of unwanted change; Oxford contrasts political and physical examples within one broad noun meaning.
lottery|random-prize-draw|a ticket-based draw in which chance determines who receives prizes|lottery ticket; win a lottery prize|A|Oxford supports buying a ticket and winning a prize, while Vocab4600 explains chance selection; keep this noun definition neutral and descriptive.
cake|sweet-baked-dessert|a sweet baked food often served as dessert or for a celebration|chocolate cake; share cake after dinner|A|Both sources support a sweet dessert and Oxford provides birthday and shared-cake examples; no unsupported recipe detail is needed.
bride|woman-getting-married|a woman on the occasion of her marriage|bride arrives at a ceremony; bride wears flowers|A|Oxford’s examples concern a bride arriving at a ceremony and wearing flowers; author a single wedding-related noun sense.
doubt|noun-uncertainty|a feeling that something may not be true or is uncertain|doubt about accuracy; have doubts|A|Oxford and Vocab4600 both describe uncertainty; the noun is kept separate from the verb used to express disbelief.
doubt|verb-question-truth|feel uncertain about or believe something may not be true|doubt that a train will arrive; doubt a claim|B|Oxford directly uses doubt that for an expected event; this verb construction expresses uncertainty rather than naming the feeling.
rifle|shoulder-fired-long-gun|a long firearm designed to be aimed from the shoulder|historical rifle display; rifle in a museum|A|Oxford identifies the object as a rifle and mentions a historical display; use a neutral museum example without handling or operation instructions.
printing|production-of-printed-material|the process of producing text or images on paper or similar material|printing of a book; cost of printing|A|Oxford examples discuss book production and its cost; define the noun as the process, not a particular printed object.
ever|at-any-time|at any time up to or during the period being discussed|have you ever seen; ever visited|A|Oxford’s question uses ever to ask whether an experience happened at any time; the adverb commonly appears with perfect forms.
ever|superlative-of-all-time|used after a superlative to emphasize a comparison with all previous times|best day ever; greatest ever|B|Oxford directly provides best day ever; retain this familiar emphatic use separately from asking whether something happened at any time.
glide|fly-with-little-effort|move through the air on extended wings with little effort|glide through the sky; glide without flapping|A|Vocab4600 defines this bird-related flying movement and supplies an example; do not extend the draft to skating or other motion.
